@page "/field-state"

<PageTitle>Field-state visualizer</PageTitle>
<h1>Field-state visualizer</h1>
<p>Each class Formidable puts on a field to show its state is decided from one small record, <code>FieldState</code>, which the table under the fields shows live for <strong>Username</strong> and <strong>Display name</strong>. Leaving a box marks its field touched here, but only a field that would pass a submit turns green.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Handle.cs", "FieldStateVisualizer.razor", "FieldStateVisualizer.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at <strong>Form valid</strong> and <strong>Submit</strong> before typing anything.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Form valid: No", and <strong>Submit</strong> disabled.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> The form's <code>TrackFormValidity</code> option checks the whole form as it opens; <strong>Username</strong> is required.</div></li>
            <li>Click into <strong>Username</strong>, then press Tab without typing.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> <strong>Username</strong>'s row reads Touched <code>True</code> and Modified <code>False</code>, and its box stays uncoloured.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> This page calls <code>MarkTouched()</code> when you leave a box, even with nothing typed.</div></li>
            <li>Press Tab again to leave <strong>Display name</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> <strong>Display name</strong> turns green, and its row reads Touched <code>True</code> and Modified <code>False</code>.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> Green means a field would pass a submit, and nothing fails for <strong>Display name</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Type <code>admin</code> into <strong>Username</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> <strong>Username</strong>'s row reads Modified <code>True</code> and Validating <code>True</code> while "checking…" shows, then Errors <code>True</code> with "That username is taken".</div></li>
            <li>Clear <strong>Username</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Username is required" at once, with no submit pressed.</div></li>
            <li>Type <code>ada</code> into <strong>Username</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "checking…", then "Username is required" clears, <strong>Form valid</strong> reads "Yes" and <strong>Submit</strong> is enabled.</div></li>
            <li>Press Tab.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> <strong>Username</strong> turns green.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> A box with focus wears the blue focus border, whatever its state.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> both rows read Validating <code>True</code> together, then "Submitted" in the status line.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> A submit checks every field at once, not only the one you edited.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Username</strong>: required; not taken (<code>admin</code>, <code>root</code> and <code>formidable</code> are).</li>
            <li><strong>Display name</strong>: optional; if filled in, not taken (<code>Administrator</code>, <code>Root User</code> and <code>Formidable</code> are).</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>Each row of the table reads one field's <code>FieldState</code>, the record its classes are decided from:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>The <strong>Touched</strong> column turns <code>True</code> when a change to the field reaches the form (from the first keystroke here) or the page calls <code>MarkTouched()</code>. A load of saved values, as on the <strong>Loading a saved draft</strong> page, touches the fields it checks that hold a value and leaves empty ones alone.</li>
            <li>The <strong>Modified</strong> column reads the form's <code>EditContext</code>: has the value changed?</li>
            <li>The <strong>Validating</strong> column is <code>True</code> while a check of that field runs.</li>
            <li>The <strong>Errors</strong> and <strong>Warnings</strong> columns say whether the field has an error, or a warning, showing right now.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>As you type, <strong>Validating</strong> turns <code>True</code> only on the field you are editing, even though one check answers every field you have changed. That holds after a submit too. A submit checks every field, so every row turns <code>True</code>. The form's invisible <code>TrackFormValidity</code> check, described below, never sets the column.</p>
        <p>The class Formidable adds while a check runs (<code>formidable-pending</code>, which the sample draws as a spinner in the box) follows the same record's <strong>Validating</strong> flag.</p>
        <p>Each input sits in a <code>FormidableField</code>, a component that wraps one field's markup. The <code>FormidableField</code> tells the form the field is on the page, and hands that markup the field's state and a few calls, under the name <code>field</code>.</p>
        <p>Leaving a box has no column and no flag of its own. On this page each input sets <code>@@onblur="() =&gt; field.MarkTouched()"</code>, a call meant for custom inputs, so leaving the box marks the field touched.</p>
        <p>A Formidable input on its own touches a field only when a change reaches the form, whether the input sends each keystroke or waits until you leave the box (its <code>UpdateOn</code> setting). So without the page's call, tabbing through a box never touches it.</p>
        <p>Green means a field would pass a submit, not merely that you visited it. A field turns green only once its <strong>Touched</strong> or <strong>Modified</strong> column reads <code>True</code>. Tabbing through <strong>Username</strong> leaves it uncoloured: the form already knows its required rule fails, though no message shows. A field that would fail a submit is never green. A box with focus wears the blue focus border, whatever its state, so green shows once you leave the box.</p>
        <p><strong>Form valid</strong> reads <code>IsFormValid</code> from the form's validation state, which the form's <code>TrackFormValidity</code> option keeps current. The option runs an invisible check of the whole form against the rules a submit checks: once when the form opens, and again on every change. That check shows no message and no "checking…". <strong>Submit</strong> stays disabled until the whole form would pass.</p>
        <p>What the option adds is the <strong>Form valid</strong> answer. That is how a fresh form reads "Form valid: No" truthfully.</p>
        <p>The option matters less for green than you might expect. Every edit's own check already runs the rules a submit checks. The form also re-checks itself against those rules when it opens and whenever a field appears or leaves the page, with or without the option. So a field you have only tabbed through turns green even with the option off.</p>
        <p>After an edit, the option's check and the edit's own check can each turn a field green, and whichever answers first does.</p>
        <p>A profile is a named selection of a validator's rules. <code>LiveProfile</code> is the profile the form checks as you edit, and left unset it is the submit profile (the rules a submit checks), as on this page.</p>
        <p>A form narrows <code>LiveProfile</code> by setting a profile with fewer rules, as the <strong>Server round-trip</strong> page does. That form's checks as you edit then skip some of a submit's rules, so they cannot show that a field would pass a submit. Before the first submit, a field on such a form turns green only through a check of the whole form: the option's check, a load of saved values, or the form's own re-check.</p>
        <p>A validator Formidable has to run whole, rather than rule by rule, is the same. A check as you edit never turns its fields green, so before a submit only those three checks of the whole form can. One example is a FluentValidation validator set to stop at its first failing rule (<code>ClassLevelCascadeMode.Stop</code>). The docs page How the engine works explains the option's invisible check.</p>
        <p>A load of saved values (<code>DiscloseLoadedValuesAsync</code>) leaves every row's <strong>Validating</strong> <code>False</code> while it runs. A load answers for the whole form, so while it runs only the form's own <code>IsValidating</code> flag is <code>True</code>, for a page-level spinner.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_handle" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary />
            </div>

            <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
                <div class="field">
                    <label>Username <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _handle.Username" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.Username" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" @onblur="() => field.MarkTouched()" /></label>
                    <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
                </div>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.Username" />
            </FormidableField>

            <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
                <div class="field">
                    <label>Display name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.DisplayName" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" @onblur="() => field.MarkTouched()" /></label>
                    <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
                </div>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.DisplayName" />
            </FormidableField>

            <table class="state-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Field</th>
                        <th>Touched</th>
                        <th>Modified</th>
                        <th>Validating</th>
                        <th>Errors</th>
                        <th>Warnings</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
                        <tr>
                            <td>Username</td>
                            <td>@field.State.IsTouched</td>
                            <td>@field.State.IsModified</td>
                            <td>@field.State.IsValidating</td>
                            <td>@field.State.HasErrors</td>
                            <td>@field.State.HasWarnings</td>
                        </tr>
                    </FormidableField>
                    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
                        <tr>
                            <td>Display name</td>
                            <td>@field.State.IsTouched</td>
                            <td>@field.State.IsModified</td>
                            <td>@field.State.IsValidating</td>
                            <td>@field.State.HasErrors</td>
                            <td>@field.State.HasWarnings</td>
                        </tr>
                    </FormidableField>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>Form valid: <strong>@(_form?.Engine?.IsFormValid == true ? "Yes" : "No")</strong></p>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="submit" disabled="@(_form?.Engine?.IsFormValid != true)">Submit</button>
            </div>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>

        <p><small>The table rows above are not a read-only view added on the side. Each row is its own <code>FormidableField</code>, the component that wraps one field's markup, just as each input sits in one. A <code>FormidableField</code> tells the form its field is on the page and re-renders whenever the form's state changes, so a table row shows its field's state the moment that state changes.</small></p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
